; The crossings a fold could not give you: a count, a magnitude, and a grid.
;
; `[count]` is the size of a sample without reading it — the piece a mean needs
; and a fold cannot supply. `[round]` is the way back down from exactness:
; Newton's method on nine converges to a value that IS three to eleven figures
; and prints as a ratio of eleven-digit integers, and the caller names the grid
; rather than the plane choosing one.
;
; Mean of 2, 4, 9 is 5; the rounded Newton value is 3. All of it free.
; expect: calls 1
; expect: clean
; expect: contains 5 3
; expect: answers
((# std/measure)
 ($ "Reply with exactly the two numbers below, separated by one space, and nothing else.\n"
    |([product] ([sum] 2 4 9) ([reciprocal] ([count] 2 4 9)))|
    " "
    (std-coarse (std-divide 76293945313 25431315104) 100)))
